SM(556,{"n":"2 Corinthians 1","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/2 Corinthians 1.mp3","d":2491.7,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.6,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[24.4,"And uh when we really fully get started, we're starting St."],[29.04,"Paul's second epistle to the Corinthians this evening."],[32.4,"And before we start, start, obviously I don't have a lot of proper recap to do to catch us up to where we are, because we're at verse one, but I am going to as always do sort of an introduction to the book."],[47.6,"It's gonna be a little different than some of the others because, of cour,se this is Second Corinthians, and we just went through First Corinthians."],[55.04,"So uh everything I said in my introduction to First Corinthians about Corinth. and all that, which is available on Ancient Faith to go back and listen to that introduction"],[65.96,"Obviously, it's the same city and uh it has the same history."],[70.76,"St. Paul is the same person writing to them."],[73.32,"Uh, and as we're gonna see once we get into the epistle, he's having to write them again because they are having uh many of the same problems."],[83.32,"They have continued and in some cases gotten worse."],[86.52,"And so the couple of things I want to talk about in this introduction, though, are first of all, I just refer to it as St."],[94.44,"Paul's second epistle to the Corinthians, but as we already talked a little bit about as we were going through 1 Corinthians, it's actually not the second letter that he wrote to the Corinthians."],[104.36,"It's actually at least the fourth letter that he wrote to the Corinthians."],[109.68,"As we saw in 1 Corinthians, when we were on our way through, he makes reference to a previous letter that he had written to them."],[118.32,"So 1 Corinthians was actually the second letter that he wrote."],[122.88,"And there's another letter in between 1 Corinthians and 2 Corinthians that he's going to refer to as 2 Corinthians, which St."],[133.12,"Paul refers to as his sorrowful letter, is how he refers to it."],[138.08,"And uh the reason he refers to it as that uh we can kind of deduce from what he said in First Corinthians."],[145.6,"Remember, at the beginning and the end of First Corinthians, St."],[148.72,"Paul mentioned that he was planning to come and visit them soon, and uh that he hoped that it would be a good visit and not a bad visit."],[158.68,"And that was part of the reason that he wrote First Corinthians was to try to guide them in taking care of some of the issues they were having before he got there."],[168.12,"That was his hope, uh, that he wouldn't have to come and deal with them himself in person, because they would have already been dealt with."],[174.84,"And as we're gonna find out on our way through 2 Corinthians, the visit did not go well, and after that visit, and that visit not going well, St."],[185.08,"Paul wrote what he referred to as his sorrowful letter expressing that, and now sometime after that, so we're sometime probably in the mid 50s.s, around 55 or 56, now he' gonna write what is 2 Corinthians"],[201.44,"And now that I've said that, I have to say it's only sort of that he's going to write 2 Corinthians."],[208.88,"Because 2 Corinthians itself is not just one letter."],[212.88,"2 Corinthians, as we're gonna see as we go through, is at least two letters, and I think probably just two, that have been kind of stitched together, kind of put together in one place."],[224.48,"The reason why it's pretty clear that that happened is that as we're gonna see as we go through, there are places where St."],[231.12,"Paul sort of repeats himself, sort of he'll he'll make an argument, you know, and then a little while later he'll make the exact same argument, not with the exact same words, like it was you know, copy pasted, but it's like he just said he just said that, and it happens more than once."],[249.56,"And so that those repetitions and that kind of thing kind of make it clear that there were originally two letters, all the material from those letters has been put together here and is being called Second Corinthians."],[263.0,"Now, if you go and get a scholarly commentary that's that thick, about 1 and 2 Corinthians, uh, there are people out there who have tried to divvy it up, divvy up 2 Corinthians into like six different letters."],[276.64,"And that kind of thing, but that's just because people need to write dissertations."],[281.8,"People need to publish articles."],[283.88,"And nobody wants to publish an article or accept a dissertation where you're just agreeing with what everyone else has said in the past."],[290.28,"So you got to come up with something new."],[292.36,"But it is it is pretty clear that we are dealing with material from two different letters that's been brought together here."],[300.28,"And it's likely that there was at least one more letter from St."],[303.64,"Paul after this."],[305.16,"So it seems that in all St."],[307.88,"Paul wrote at least six letters to the church in Corinth."],[314.04,"And sadly, even those six letters don't seem to have solved all the problems."],[320.96,"Because at the end of the search first century in the 90s, St."],[324.68,"Clement of Rome is writing another letter to Corinth to try to. help them resolve the issues"],[332.08,"So Corinth had a lot of problems sort of throughout the first century and into the second century."],[339.6,"And as we went through 1 Corinthians, we talked a lot about what those problems were, and that they came, they came chiefly under the heading of the church which was primarily made up of Gentile converts, primarily Greek ethnic converts, was not really making a clean break with their previous pagan Greek culture."],[364.64,"So they were trying to either bring some of their worship practices and lifestyle practices with them into the church, or they kind of had one foot in both worlds."],[376.92,"They'd come to the church on one day and then go down to the temple of Demeter down the street, you know, the next day."],[385.61,"And they didn't understand, and St."],[388.17,"Paul was trying to make clear to them, you know, no, you know, things are different now, now that you've come to Christ."],[395.05,"And so the way you live your life, your behavior, your moral behavior needs to change."],[400.33,"The worship of God is not like the worship of the demons you used to worship."],[406.09,"This is all different now, and there needs to be a clean break."],[409.77,"You need to be holy, you need to be set apart from sort of the rest of the rest of the culture now."],[416.41,"And we talked about how that was not an easy thing for them to do."],[418.57,"I mean, this meant opting out of the whole life of the city"],[423.25,"This meant opting out of chunks of their family life that they could no longer participate in."],[429.33,"This would cause great difficulties for them, and that's why so many of them were trying sort of not to do it."],[434.61,"We're sort of trying to be Christian, but still sort of fly under the radar and not have to really make hard make hard choices."],[443.73,"So the last thing I wanted to talk about by way of introduction was to go into a little more about the fact that 2 Corinthians is two letters."],[453.09,"Because that may not bother anyone in this room, but there are a lot of people who that deeply bothers."],[459.23,"The idea that that what we have in the Bible is 2 Corinthians is something that was put together by somebody other than St."],[463.15,"Paul later on from things that St"],[470.47,"Paul wrote. And this is the reason this causes some people trouble, especially in the United States, some of our Christian friends, especially outside the Orthodox Church, is that starting in the 19th century, there came to be a big emphasis, particularly in uh in the Protestant world, on the idea of quote unquote the originals of the books, especially of the New Testament."],[504.71,"And the reason for that was that in the 19th century, a, a lot of people, there were shift, various shifts in society and in in scholarship and that kind of thing that led to lot of people starting to question the Bible, trying to find errors in the Bible, contradictions in the Bible, pointing to all the differences between the gospels and saying, Oh, well, see, there's all these differences, so you can't trust any of it, that sort of thing."],[529.87,"And so, in order to defend against that, uh, a number of more traditional Protestant scholars in the United States."],[539.43,"One of the ways they defended against that, I should say, is that they said, Well, you know, what we have now has been copied how many thousands of times, you know, in the last uh at that time, 1800 years."],[552.45,"And so we believed that you know they they said that they believed that God uh inspired the sort of the originals."],[560.19,"When St. Paul wrote it down, every word was perfect, it was exactly the word God wanted, you know, and so if there are discrepancies now, a lot of those things are things that sort of crept in while people were copying and recopying and recopying and recopying."],[576.19,"And you could understand how discrepancies creep in if you've ever sat down and tried to copy something, you know."],[582.27,"Sit down sometimes and try and copy 10 pages of something and then go back and compare and see if you made any mistakes."],[588.33,"And then imagine if someone took your copy and made a copy of that, and then someone else took that and made a copy of that."],[596.37,"That's what English meant in the Right."],[598.94,"Yeah. There was no printing press."],[600.54,"So that's the only way you could keep keep things going is manuscripts aged and stuff."],[605.34,"So uh but that process is sort of fraught with that, you know."],[608.46,"And then five or six people down the chain, they'd see something and say, Oh, that has to be an error."],[612.78,"And so they correct it."],[614.86,"But they were correcting it based on what they thought it should be."],[618.56,"They didn't have they didn't have anything to compare it to."],[621.04,"So you can understand how that would happen."],[622.72,"But so that position shifted the way people, especially in America, thought about the Bible."],[630.56,"Before that, the debate had always been about any debates about the Bible, had been about what text that we had received from history. was the right one that we should be reading"],[644.04,"So you go back to the Protestant Reformation in the 16th century, Rome said the Latin Vulgate, as it existed at that time, that was it."],[653.4,"That was the one we should be using."],[655.64,"Whereas most of the Protestant reformers said, no, we should be going back to Greek and Hebrew, and they were doing that based on a relatively small group of manuscripts."],[670.92,"Erasmus had, I think, eight."],[673.72,"That said he was missing part of Revelation."],[676.04,"He had to back translate it from Latin back into Greek because he couldn't find a Greek copy of it."],[682.68,"But so they argued you should go back to that."],[684.6,"So those were the debates from the 16th century until the 19th century."],[689.76,"So in the 19th century, you get, well, no, we need to try to get back to the original."],[695.36,"And at that time they had access to a lot more manuscripts."],[698.48,"We today have access to about 6,000 of the New Testament."],[703.12,"And so you will hear people very confidently say, especially in Protestant biblical scholarship, very confidently say, we could know for sure what the original said because we have all these copies."],[713.06,"And now we especially we could feed all those copies, all those manuscripts into computers."],[718.28,"And let the computers date them and figure out what was copied from what, and they can, you know, break it all down."],[725.64,"Here's the problem."],[728.06,"Here's the problem."],[729.18,"It's unclear in a lot of cases what the original means."],[729.9,"So taking St"],[736.58,"Paul's epistles as an example, the way St."],[740.02,"Paul wrote his epistles, we think, well, St."],[742.34,"Paul wrote it, so he must have sat down with like a parchment and written it out."],[747.46,"He didn't. He couldn't."],[750.18,"He had eye problems."],[751.54,"In fact, there are several points in his epistles where he'll say that he's writing something with his own hand, and a couple of times he makes kind of comments like, see with what large letters I write, because he's writing it with his own with his own hand, so it's big and messy, or he'll sort of sign it, you know."],[765.86,"Um, but so the way the way letters were typically written in the ancient world, and the way the epistles in in the New Testament, at least for the most part, were written, was that you would have someone who was called an emanuensis in Greek, it's basically a secretary or a scribe, because most people were illiterate."],[784.3,"So if you could read and write, that put you in this special class, then you could hire yourself out and get make a lot of money by by writing for people."],[795.74,"And so uh St."],[797.18,"Paul would have dictated his letter to this person who would have written it down, but it wouldn't have been just one for one, like he's writing down the exact words, even"],[809.55,"Because he could spoken and written language is different."],[813.93,"So he'd be making little correct grammatical corrections and that kind of thing."],[817.95,"As he wrote."],[819.83,"Then after the scribe was done, that would be given back to the author, like given back to St."],[825.67,"Paul, and Saint Paul would have read over it and checked it."],[829.93,"So that if the secretary had made any little tweaks like that and he didn't like them."],[834.35,"He could say, No, I meant, you know, whatever."],[837.39,"Make any changes he wanted."],[840.27,"And then that letter would have been given to another person who would have taken it to the church in question, because again, most of them couldn't read."],[850.59,"So would have taken it to the church in question and read it to them out loud, and then been prepared to answer, to answer questions about it."],[859.31,"So in this case, what's the original?"],[861.71,"Is it what St."],[862.35,"Paul said when he dictated it the first time?"],[862.51,"Is it what the secretary wrote"],[868.71,"Is it what was left after he checked what the secretary wrote?"],[872.31,"And then around 100 AD, someone took all of St."],[876.79,"Paul's epistles and put them together into a collection."],[881.35,"And every copy of St."],[882.87,"Paul's letters that we have is from that collection."],[886.63,"We don't have a single manuscript from a separate epistle."],[892.79,"So is the original the first copy of that collection?"],[897.6,"And if this is why it causes a problem with 2 Corinthians, what's the original with 2 Corinthians?"],[903.68,"The two separate letters in substate or the or the first time they were put together when they were put together in the uh collection."],[914.7,"That that version."],[917.1,"And these problems just get magnified a hundredfold when you get into the Old Testament."],[923.02,"Because most of the Old Testament, we don't know who wrote it."],[925.52,"There's there's nobody who claims to have written first and second Samuel and first and second Kings."],[931.6,"Every once in a while, some people will say, oh, well, it's first and second Samuel, so Samuel must have written it."],[936.0,"Well, Samuel dies in 1 Samuel 23, which would have made it really hard for him to write 2 Samuel."],[942.08,"Minimally. So the these books, and who wrote Judges doesn't say."],[947.73,"Who wrote Ruth doesn't say Ruth is about Ruth."],[950.61,"First and second chronicles, we have no idea"],[951.23,"It doesn't say she wrote it."],[956.88,"Most of the books of the prophets, like you say Isaiah, well, that was that written by Isaiah?"],[962.0,"Well, in the book of Isaiah, it talks about how Isaiah's disciples wrote down and collected his prophecies and put them into books."],[970.64,"So that's what the book of Isaiah is."],[972.8,"And that's why there are sort of parts of the book."],[975.28,"You'll sometimes hear people referring to different books inside the book of Isaiah."],[980.0,"That's because it came from separate collections of his prophecies that were later all put together into one big collection."],[986.8,"So, what's the original in that case?"],[989.68,"What does the original even mean in that case?"],[992.56,"And that's why unfortunately a lot of our Protestant friends have gotten locked into these huge arguments about Moses writing the Torah, writing the Pentateuch."],[1003.72,"You know, well, if you if you read the Torah, the first thing you'll notice is that it's written in a language, the oldest language we have it in."],[1011.02,"Hebrew, biblical Hebrew."],[1012.86,"That's a language that didn't exist when Moses was alive."],[1016.78,"So he can't have actually written that."],[1019.44,"Any more than he could have written it in Greek."],[1021.73,"Because language wasn't there."],[1024.21,"And he would have been writing on tablets in the desert."],[1028.31,"So what Moses wrote, I'm not saying Moses didn't write anything, but what Moses wrote would look very different than what we have now."],[1036.95,"The content may be. the same content, but it would look very different"],[1042.27,"And then you have problems like the fact that it talks about Moses dying, which obviously he didn't write."],[1049.47,"And you have the problem that all through it there are places where somebody has updated place names, and they say they're doing it."],[1056.75,"Yeah, they say it's now called such and names."],[1058.59,"Yeah, it's now called such and such."],[1060.83,"Well, Moses wouldn't have written that."],[1063.16,"Moses would be prophetically saying, oh, someday it will be called, you know, whatever."],[1068.78,"So someone's come through and edited it."],[1071.4,"Clearly, and at least updated those names."],[1073.82,"And someone must have translated it into the, you know, effectively translated it into the form we have now and done editing and and that. kind of thing"],[1082.9,"Um my point here is just if if you go down this rabbit hole of the original."],[1089.91,"In a lot of cases, with like with the Old Testament, it's stuff we don't even have anymore."],[1093.83,"The original. I mean, the thing that Moses actually wrote, we don't have anymore."],[1098.87,"We have a later version of it."],[1101.44,"Of what Moses wrote."],[1103.2,"So uh that's a that's a rabbit hole we can't go down talking about the original."],[1110.08,"There's another problem with that, because um, and there there's there's a very major evangelical Protestant statement on the inerrancy of the Bible, on the fact that the Bible doesn't contain errors."],[1121.97,"Uh called the Nashville Statement on inerrancy."],[1125.45,"And it says in it regarding the Bible being without errors and the Bible being inspired by God, it says that it's inspired in the autographs."],[1136.35,"The original. In the originals, which they say we can now establish using modern methods and computer tools."],[1145.17,"It's like, well, that sounds great."],[1148.13,"Except that means that for 1900 years, give or take, nobody had a Bible that didn't have errors in it."],[1159.03,"If you take that, if you take that seriously."],[1162.11,"That means all the church fathers, all the Protestant reformers, you know, whoever you want to go to, whoever's on your team in church history, none of them had a all of their Bibles were full of errors and weren't the version that was inspired by God that whole time."],[1179.99,"Which is another separate problem."],[1182.15,"Even beyond the fact that they're talking about the originals, and you know, that only even makes sense with the New Testament."],[1188.87,"Computer search tools don't help us at all on the Old Testament."],[1192.01,"In terms of quote unquote figuring out what the original is."],[1195.77,"So we need to set aside this whole idea of the originals."],[1202.65,"Because even those folks who I'm talking about will say that the same Holy Spirit who inspired the scriptures, who inspired the Bible originally, also was guiding the whole process for thousands of years that brought it down to us."],[1221.57,"That he was active in that, preserving the scriptures, making sure they were copied, making sure they were preserved, making sure that the church picked the right ones, to have."],[1242.39,"Holy Spirit did that, but then they'll say, but all these errors crept in."],[1247.91,"So that to me sounds like you're saying, Well, the Holy Spirit was trying to keep it perfect and somehow failed."],[1256.31,"So I come at it from the other direction."],[1258.47,"I come at it from the direction that whatever the Holy Spirit was trying to do, the Holy Spirit's God."],[1264.47,"So whatever God sets out to do, He does."],[1267.59,"So whatever happened historically with the text of the Scriptures must have been exactly what the Holy Spirit wanted to happen."],[1278.23,"So rather than worrying about the original, like this is the perfect version and it's lost somewhere back there in the mists of history."],[1288.01,"Every jet in every generation of the church, every generation of God's people, going back into the Old Testament when they had the Torah, and that was it."],[1295.69,"Every generation of God's people has had the scriptures that God wanted them to have."],[1300.89,"St They've had the scriptures that were theirs."],[1305.49,"John Chrysostom. His New Testament text was the New Testament."],[1311.94,"It was the scriptures God wanted him to have in his hands at that time."],[1317.46,"And so what that means is sort of the bounty that we have today, when we do have 6,000 different New Testament manuscripts, when we do have Greek and Hebrew and Aramaic versions of the Old Testament, where we do have all these sort of riches of knowledge available to us, and we have the computer tools to be able to, you know, put them all together and compare them and find all these things."],[1342.34,"That means we've been given sort of this big gift by God that previous generations didn't have uh in in having all that."],[1349.98,"Not to mention having the printing press, being able to have three and four Bibles laying around our house gathering dust."],[1356.47,"Which no previous generation of Christians has had."],[1359.43,"Being actually being able to read them, you know, having literature a literacy rate, you know, above single digits."],[1374.05,"And so rather than trying to uh defend defend them by digging back into this archaeology and these hypothetical perfect versions, we need to sort of embrace what we have."],[1389.95,"And be thankful for it and study it and learn as much as we can from it."],[1395.79,"And when we if we look and we see that guess what, 2 Corinthians looks like looks like it's two different letters edited together, then that means that the Holy Spirit wanted the material from those two letters to be put together and handed down to us."],[1413.63,"And what whatever was in that first letter, St."],[1416.35,"Paul wrote to Corinth, and whatever was in that sorrowful third letter, didn't want us to have."],[1421.87,"For whatever reason"],[1422.81,"If we found them tomorrow, we wouldn't stick them in the Bible."],[1425.91,"Because they're not what uh they're not what God wanted us to have."],[1430.79,"Uh, so anyway, I wanted to comment on that since we're starting 2 Corinthians, and because when recordings of this go out far and like I said, nob nobody in this room may have a big issue with it being two letters edited together."],[1443.27,"But when this goes out far and wide, and we do have we do have people in our modern world who appear on documentaries on the History Channel and National Geographic Channel and who go on NPR for interviews and stuff who try to use stuff like this to discredit the Bible, or at least to put doubts in people's head, people who don't know about it, or people who have the wrong idea."],[1465.12,"People who think like St."],[1466.4,"Paul sat and wrote something down, and that's exactly what I have in front of me now."],[1469.84,"And so someone comes and casts some doubt on that, and people lose their faith or start to doubt or wander off, or those things pile up."],[1478.08,"And so that's why I wanted to talk about it"],[1484.2,"Even if no one here in this room is one of those people."],[1486.76,"Hopefully, when this goes out."],[1488.28,"If there are any folks for whom that would be a problem, uh they will be able to hear that."],[1494.84,"So, unless anybody has any comments or questions or insinuations to make, uh, we'll go ahead and get started in uh St."],[1504.36,"Paul's second epistle to the Corinthians, chapter one, verse one."],[1508.44,"So it begins, Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy, our brother, to the church of God, which is at Corinth, with all the saints who are in all Achaia."],[1521.72,"So Achaea is the the province, Roman province in which Corinth is."],[1527.62,"So one of us all Greece is."],[1530.74,"Yeah. Most, well, yeah, depending on where you draw the border of Greece."],[1535.06,"Yeah. You draw the border of Greece."],[1538.5,"And uh so St."],[1541.14,"Paul is expecting this letter to circulate a little bit."],[1544.18,"Okay, this is another important point."],[1546.42,"Of course, St."],[1546.98,"Paul is writing it directly to this church to talk to them about their problems, but he's not expecting it to sort of be delivered there, be read once, and then, you know, okay."],[1559.14,"And then moving on."],[1560.26,"He's expecting this letter to be passed around to other churches for other people to hear it, and eventually it being collected with the other letters is not something that Saint Paul wouldn't have foreseen."],[1567.62,"Or think was odd"],[1573.96,"And so he identifies himself the way he typically does."],[1578.18,"Yes, sir. Timothy's included here."],[1580.42,"What? Yes. I'm about to get to that."],[1585.86,"But so he identifies himself as an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God."],[1590.82,"So apostle, remember, is uh the word for someone who's sent."],[1596.64,"Apostolos. It's someone who's sent, particularly with a with a mission."],[1601.45,"And uh that's twofold."],[1603.41,"It's he's got a particular mission, of course, that St."],[1605.77,"Paul's been sent on, a particular calling."],[1608.25,"Um, but I've also mentioned before, I think that the Greek word apostolos uh is covering not covering, but in the Greek of the Old Testament is used to translate the Hebrew word uh shalioch, which means the same thing."],[1624.61,"It means somebody who's sent."],[1626.98,"But uh that's its strict definition."],[1629.38,"But it came in its normal uh Hebrew and Aramaic use to refer to someone who's sent as a representative, uh, which is um like an ambassador."],[1642.34,"Uh more like a lawyer."],[1645.3,"My representation. It was someone who you'd send to represent you in terms of legal matters."],[1651.14,"Negotiating some kind of contract or business deal or that kind of thing."],[1654.9,"Sort of the way we'd use a lawyer now"],[1658.38,"So yeah, it was less like an ambassador, more like my personal representative who I'm going to send, or power of attorney kind of idea."],[1665.12,"This person's going to come and represent my interests, in what this matter is."],[1669.29,"Uh and so that's important in terms of St."],[1672.97,"Paul's self-understanding of what an apostle is."],[1675.71,"Of what an apostle is."],[1676.75,"It's not just someone sent, but that when he's speaking to the Church of God at Corinth, he has this self-consciousness that he's being sent, the issues he's going to try to resolve."],[1690.93,"He has this authority that comes from Christ himself."],[1694.55,"Because he's an apostle of Jesus Christ."],[1697.11,"That he has this. this authority from Christ Himself to come and resolve resolve matter"],[1703.47,"And there's even an element of that."],[1706.03,"Remember how he began 1 Corinthians talking about how he was coming on this visit and how he wanted it to be a good visit."],[1714.21,"And not a bad visit."],[1715.41,"Well, you have to remember key to St."],[1718.05,"Paul's theology here, and we saw it back in 1 Corinthians 15, is the resurrection and the return of Christ."],[1725.41,"This is a major part of how St."],[1727.49,"Paul sees what he's doing and going and preaching the gospel of Jesus Christ."],[1733.21,"We mentioned before that the word evangelion that's translated as gospel was usually used in the plural in the ancient world."],[1742.57,"And the evangelia in the plural of the emperor or a general or a Roman official would be read out publicly before his visit."],[1752.41,"It would be all his triumphs and victories and achievements would all be read out."],[1757.29,"And it was read out with the idea that the people of that city would hear, who it was who was coming to visit them and would therefore prepare appropriately."],[1768.9,"And so this is part of a key part of how St."],[1771.7,"Paul understands his preaching the gospel to, for example, Corinth."],[1776.1,"He's preaching the gospel of Jesus Christ."],[1778.8,"The evangelion of Jesus Christ."],[1781.16,"Who he is, what he's done, his victories, what he's accomplished, so that they can prepare for his return."],[1786.32,"they So can prepare for his coming."],[1792.44,"So that when he comes, it will be a good visit for them and not a bad visit for them."],[1797.02,"So that they can do what they need to do."],[1799.5,"And so that's also entailed here, just in St."],[1802.54,"Paul's self-identification, that he's an apostle of Jesus Christ."],[1806.22,"That's why he's here and he's doing this."],[1808.38,"He's trying to help them resolve these things, get things together, because, as St."],[1813.82,"Paul said at the end of 1 Corinthians, Christ is coming soon."],[1817.18,"And they need to be, they need to be ready for that."],[1821.74,"And so I notice he says that he's an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God."],[1827.26,"This is this is something I've recently been reminded to reiterate."],[1831.18,"When St Paul says just God, to make sure everybody's clear, he's referring to God the Father when he just says God."],[1839.62,"That's going to be reiterated to us when we get to chapter 6, which will be a while."],[1843.14,"But when we get to chapter 6, he's going to reiterate that."],[1847.78,"So we have this is sort of a partially Trinitarian."],[1851.46,"He doesn't mention the the Holy Spirit here in particular."],[1856.1,"But he's an apostle of Jesus Christ by the by the will of God."],[1859.78,"And he adds this, and Timothy, our brother."],[1863.54,"Another thing that has uh generated a lot of dissertations and articles is which of St."],[1870.9,"Paul's epistles did St."],[1872.58,"Paul actually write."],[1874.66,"So there are seven that sort of everybody agrees St."],[1876.1,"Paul wrote And then there are people out there who question all the other ones."],[1885.42,"And the answer to most of these questions is sort of right in front of us in a lot of cases."],[1891.5,"So there's a whole series of these, including some of the ones that people doubt, where right here at the beginning, St."],[1899.26,"Paul mentions a co-author, usually Timothy."],[1902.22,"So if the Greek is a little different than St."],[1905.42,"Paul's usual Greek, and at the beginning of it, he said it was a letter from St."],[1909.82,"Paul and this other person."],[1911.86,"What would be the natural conclusion?"],[1914.14,"If the Greek's a little different, that's because there was this other person involved."],[1917.66,"Seems kind of simple."],[1919.42,"But you can't get a dissertation out of that."],[1921.18,"You can't get a published academic article out of simple"],[1926.99,"So that's that's the reality."],[1928.83,"On several of these, St."],[1929.87,"Paul has co-authors."],[1932.03,"And we get more toward some of them that sort of everybody doubts, like Hebrews."],[1937.95,"We'll talk more about that because there's a number of the later epistles of St."],[1942.83,"Paul, like Hebrews, where a lot of the Greek that doesn't look like St."],[1947.39,"Paul's looks a whole lot like St."],[1949.23,"Luke's in the Gospel of Luke and uh Acts."],[1952.51,"But we'll get to that."],[1953.71,"But so yes, Timothy's mentioned there because he's uh a co he's being listed as a co-author."],[1961.31,"And there is no way to know exactly how much input he had in any given."],[1964.83,"Yeah. Or even if he's just the one who was doing the writing."],[1972.77,"Like the physical writing in some cases."],[1975.73,"Uh in some cases, like we saw at the end of Romans."],[1979.18,"I Tertius write this to you."],[1981.44,"So we know that whoever uh whoever it was who was doing the actual writing, whoever the secretary was, his name was Tertius, uh, which means third."],[1989.84,"Um, but we talked about at the time."],[1991.6,"That was common for Romans because the rest the rest of your names, other than your first name, were like your father's name and your grandfather's name and that kind of thing."],[2000.32,"So a lot of fathers would just give their name, their grandfather's or their father's name, and then just number their sons, Primus, Secundus, Tertius."],[2009.12,"So that would be a uh a personal name"],[2016.28,"But so in this case, Timothy, Timothy is involved in the writing."],[2021.0,"And remember, in 1 Corinthians, Saint Paul had said that he was sending Saint Timothy ahead of him to Corinth before his visit."],[2030.04,"So Saint Timothy is someone who, and that's probably also why he has input now into this letter."],[2035.88,"Saint Timothy has been to the church on behalf of St."],[2039.96,"Paul, has also been trying to deal with these situations, has insight into the situation, and is someone who they know, which is why he would be uh involved."],[2050.92,"Then verse 2 grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ."],[2053.56,"God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ."],[2056.5,"So here we have it explicitly."],[2061.76,"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort, who comforts us in all our tribulation, that we may be able to comfort those who are in any trouble with the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God."],[2077.2,"So you can tell probably already from the tenor of this."],[2080.24,"He starts right off talking about suffering and troubles."],[2085.7,"And tribulations. So we already have a little different tenor than First Corinthians."],[2092.03,"First Corinthians St."],[2093.31,"Paul wrote a little more confidently, like, I'm hearing these bad reports, I don't like what what I'm hearing, but here's we need to do."],[2100.12,"And there's been some water under the bridge since then."],[2104.2,"And so the people there have had problems that have been through some stuff."],[2108.68,"St. Paul's been through some stuff with them."],[2110.6,"St. Timothy's been through some stuff with them."],[2112.92,"And so he starts out, and that's important, he's focusing on comfort."],[2117.48,"So he's not writing them an angry letter."],[2120.84,"What is wrong with you people?"],[2122.34,"That's not what he's."],[2124.1,"Why can't you get it through your thick skulls?"],[2126.26,"That's not where he's starting."],[2127.48,"He's starting with comfort."],[2128.78,"With comfort. With we need to, okay."],[2133.1,"Let's come back together."],[2135.27,"And let's let's try and start working through this stuff."],[2138.95,"And so he begins by telling about how God the Father."],[2144.25,"It's the Father of all mercies, who comforts us."],[2147.53,"And once God has comforted us and we've experienced his comfort, then we're able to pass that comfort on to others and to help those who are going through going through struggles and trials."],[2159.67,"So St. Paul first talked about his authority."],[2164.04,"Here's the authority I have as I come to settle this."],[2166.84,"And now he's coming at it from the other direction."],[2169.1,"To say, I've been comforted."],[2171.42,"We don't know what the tenor of the sorrowful letter was, but he's received comfort from God."],[2176.38,"He's a piece, and he wants to pass that on to them."],[2180.54,"He wants to now bring bring that peace to this situation."],[2183.18,"And as a last comment, notice the way he talks about God the Father"],[2190.1,"This is important because of some of the Western ideas about God."],[2196.46,"That tend to say, have God the Father is sort of the angry judgmental God, and then Jesus is sort of the nice and merciful God."],[2207.68,"Like, you know, and he sort of he intercedes for us."],[2210.16,"Like God the Father is really mad at us, but but Jesus comes and you know calms him down and you know stops him from punishing us for all our sins and everything."],[2221.22,"Um you can kind of get that impression, but that's not how St."],[2223.86,"Paul talks about."],[2224.76,"God the Father is the Father of all mercies."],[2226.82,"The Father is merciful, the Father is compassionate."],[2230.19,"This isn't some kind of juxtaposition between the two of them."],[2235.33,"He's not the mean Old Testament God, and now Jesus is the friendly, is the friendly New Testament God."],[2241.73,"Not for St."],[2242.29,"Paul at least."],[2243.59,"Not for St."],[2244.15,"Paul at least."],[2245.03,"Verse 5, for as the sufferings of Christ abound in us, so our consolation also abounds through Christ."],[2252.28,"So he's talking about the fact that uh our sufferings, whatever they are, in this life, are us partaking in the sufferings of Christ Himself."],[2263.09,"That's how he understands them."],[2265.01,"And that's how he understands their meaning."],[2267.49,"So what he's gone through, what the people at the church in Corinth have gone through in their struggles, to live life as Christians, is not just random occurrences or bad luck."],[2279.55,"This is because they're followers of Christ."],[2281.87,"And if we're following Christ, that means we're walking the same road he walked."],[2285.79,"And the road he walked involved a lot of rejection and a lot of suffering and a lot of grief and a lot of sorrow."],[2291.71,"So he's saying that's that's why we're experiencing these things."],[2296.03,"But but as much as we participate in his sufferings, we also experien we also uh participate and experience the consolation that comes through him."],[2307.87,"Because Christ didn't just die on the cross in horrible pain, he also rose again."],[2312.79,"And so that comfort and consolation is also something that we experience even when we're suffering."],[2320.39,"Now, if we are afflicted, it is for your consolation and salvation, which is effective for enduring the same sufferings which we also suffer."],[2329.59,"Or if we are comforted, it is for your consolation and salvation."],[2335.19,"So St. Paul's continuing the idea."],[2337.43,"He was talking about how the Father comforts us and then we can comfort others."],[2341.03,"And so he's saying, whatever I've suffered, whatever I have gone through in regard to the sort of struggle he's having with the people in Corinth, whatever suffering I've had is for uh their consolation and salvation, for helping them and endure get through the suffering."],[2358.61,"And any consolation he's received about the whole issue is something that he also wants to pass on to them."],[2368.13,"Sounds like he's referring to, or is he referring to specific things like was he in prison at this time?"],[2376.21,"Was he on his way to home?"],[2377.73,"Well, this is he's specifically talking about suffering for their sakes."],[2380.45,"So that's not yet."],[2381.57,"So, so he's yeah, so he's saying he's talking about the experience with them."],[2386.29,"Okay, which is the opposite of the way we might tend to take it."],[2390.15,"If I was assigned a priest at a church and they were doing the stuff that they were doing in Corinth, I would probably not react the way, that St."],[2399.78,"Paul. did And when they put me through a lot and I was suffering and having to deal with it, it'd be much more easy for me to blame them and dislike them than to do what St."],[2411.34,"Paul is doing and say, hey, you know, whatever I'm going through."],[2415.08,"I want it to help me help you resolve this."],[2419.64,"And whatever consolation and peace God gives me as I work through this, I want to pass on to you so that you can have it too."],[2429.08,"So St. Paul is establishing again what he's trying to do in this letter."],[2434.36,"He is coming as a representative."],[2436.2,"He is coming as a representative with authority."],[2438.94,"In First Corinthians, he had kind of a veiled threat there of I'd like this to be a good visit."],[2443.86,"R?ight That's not his approach this time."],[2444.3,"His approach this time is much more"],[2447.99,"I'm a representative, but I'm on your side in terms of wanting to resolve this."],[2453.27,"I'm not here to sort of mediate God's judgment to you and set everything in order."],[2458.31,"I'm coming here to mediate God's mercy and consolation and comfort to you for healing and repentance and restoration after everything that's happened."],[2469.35,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[2476.63,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2481.99,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":32.4,"lv":2,"h":"Introduction to 2 Corinthians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>St. Paul's second epistle to the Corinthians</b>; at v.1 so no recap - intro instead</li><li class=\"d0\">Same city, same history, same Paul - see <b>1 Corinthians intro</b> on Ancient Faith</li><li class=\"d0\">Same problems - <b>continued, some worse</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not really the \"second\" letter - at least the <b>fourth</b> to Corinth</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>previous letter</b> referred to in 1 Cor</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor = 2nd letter</li><li class=\"d1\">between them: the <b>sorrowful letter</b></li></ul>"},{"t":138.08,"lv":3,"h":"The sorrowful letter and the bad visit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why \"sorrowful\" - deduced from 1 Cor</li><li class=\"d1\">at start and end of 1 Cor Paul planned a visit, hoped for <b>good visit, not bad</b></li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor written to guide them to fix things <b>before</b> he came</li><li class=\"d0\">As we'll see: <b>the visit did not go well</b></li><li class=\"d1\">after it, Paul wrote the <b>sorrowful letter</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then 2 Corinthians, ~<b>55 or 56 AD</b> (mid 50s)</li></ul>"},{"t":201.44,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians is two letters","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Only \"sort of\" one letter - <b>at least two</b>, probably just two, <b>stitched together</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Evidence: Paul <b>repeats himself</b> - makes an argument, later makes the same one in different words</li><li class=\"d1\">more than once</li><li class=\"d0\">Scholarly commentaries divide it into <b>six letters</b> - \"people need to write dissertations\"</li><li class=\"d1\">nobody publishes agreeing with everyone else</li><li class=\"d0\">Likely <b>one more letter after</b> this</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul wrote <b>at least six</b> letters to Corinth</li><li class=\"d0\">Even six didn't fix it: <b>St. Clement of Rome</b> writes Corinth again in the <b>90s</b></li></ul>"},{"t":332.08,"lv":2,"h":"Corinth's problems","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Problems all through 1st c. into 2nd</li><li class=\"d0\">Main heading: church mostly <b>Gentile, ethnic Greek</b> converts - no <b>clean break</b> with pagan culture</li><li class=\"d1\">brought worship and lifestyle practices in</li><li class=\"d1\">\"one foot in both worlds\": church one day, <b>temple of Demeter</b> down the street the next</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: things are different now - behavior and morals must change</li><li class=\"d1\">worship of God not like worship of <b>demons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">be <b>holy</b> = set apart</li><li class=\"d0\">Not easy: <b>opting out of city life</b>, chunks of family life</li><li class=\"d1\">so many tried to \"fly under the radar\"</li></ul>"},{"t":443.73,"lv":2,"h":"Why \"two letters\" bothers some: the originals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Last intro point: the two-letter issue deeply bothers many people - esp. in the <b>US</b></li><li class=\"d1\">idea that the Bible was put together by someone <b>other than Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Roots: <b>19th c.</b> emphasis, esp. Protestant, on <b>\"the originals\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">shifts in society and scholarship; people hunting <b>errors and contradictions</b>, gospel differences</li><li class=\"d1\">traditional Protestant scholars' defense: <b>originals</b> inspired, every word perfect</li><li class=\"d1\">errors <b>crept in</b> through thousands of copies over 1800 years</li><li class=\"d0\">Copying analogy: copy 10 pages, compare; then someone copies <b>your copy</b>...</li><li class=\"d1\">no printing press</li><li class=\"d1\">5-6 copyists down, someone sees an \"error\" and <b>\"corrects\"</b> it - nothing to compare to</li></ul>"},{"t":622.72,"lv":3,"h":"How the debate used to be framed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Before: debate was <b>which received text</b> is right</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>16th c.</b> Reformation</li><li class=\"d1\">Rome: the <b>Latin Vulgate</b> as it was</li><li class=\"d1\">Reformers: back to <b>Greek and Hebrew</b>, from a small set of manuscripts</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Erasmus</b> had ~8; missing part of <b>Revelation</b> - back-translated from Latin</li><li class=\"d0\">19th c.: get back to <b>the original</b> - many more manuscripts</li><li class=\"d1\">today ~<b>6,000</b> NT manuscripts</li><li class=\"d1\">confident claim: \"we can know for sure\" - even feed them into <b>computers</b> to date and trace copying</li><li class=\"d0\">The problem: <b>unclear what \"the original\" means</b></li></ul>"},{"t":729.18,"lv":2,"h":"How Paul's letters were actually written","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul didn't sit and write on parchment - <b>eye problems</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes writes with own hand: \"see with what <b>large letters</b> I write\"; signs it</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient practice: <b>amanuensis</b> = secretary/scribe</li><li class=\"d1\">most people illiterate - literacy = special class, hired out, paid well</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>dictated</b>; scribe not word-for-word</li><li class=\"d1\">spoken vs written differ → small <b>grammatical corrections</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul reads it over, can change anything he didn't like</li><li class=\"d0\">Carried to church by another person, <b>read aloud</b>, ready to answer questions</li><li class=\"d0\">So which is \"the original\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's first dictation? the scribe's copy? after his check?</li></ul>"},{"t":872.31,"lv":2,"h":"The Pauline collection - and the Old Testament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">~<b>100 AD</b>: someone gathered all Paul's epistles into a <b>collection</b></li><li class=\"d1\">every copy we have comes from it - <b>no manuscript of a separate epistle</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Original of 2 Cor = the <b>two separate letters</b>, or the first time combined?</li><li class=\"d0\">Magnified <b>a hundredfold</b> in the Old Testament - mostly unknown authors</li><li class=\"d1\">nobody claims <b>Samuel/Kings</b>; Samuel dies in <b>1 Sam 23</b> - hard to write 2 Samuel</li><li class=\"d1\">Judges, Ruth, Chronicles - not stated</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaiah</b>: disciples wrote down and collected his prophecies → separate collections combined</li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant friends locked into arguments about <b>Moses writing the Pentateuch</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1003.72,"lv":3,"h":"Moses and the Torah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Torah is in <b>biblical Hebrew</b> - didn't exist in Moses' day</li><li class=\"d1\">couldn't be Greek either; he'd be writing on <b>tablets in the desert</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not saying Moses wrote nothing - what he wrote would <b>look very different</b></li><li class=\"d1\">content maybe the same</li><li class=\"d0\">It <b>talks about Moses dying</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Updated place names</b> - \"now called such and such\" - Moses would say \"someday it will be called\"</li><li class=\"d1\">someone edited and effectively <b>translated</b> it into the form we have</li><li class=\"d0\">The thing Moses wrote - we <b>don't have anymore</b>; later version</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Rabbit hole\" we can't go down</li></ul>"},{"t":1110.08,"lv":2,"h":"Inerrancy of the autographs","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Major evangelical statement on <b>inerrancy</b> (Fr S: \"Nashville Statement\") - inspired <b>in the autographs</b></li><li class=\"d1\">originals, which \"we can now establish\" with <b>modern methods and computer tools</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then for <b>~1900 years nobody</b> had an error-free Bible</li><li class=\"d1\">all the <b>church fathers</b>, all the <b>reformers</b> - Bibles full of errors, not the inspired version</li><li class=\"d0\">Also: only makes sense for NT</li><li class=\"d1\">computer tools <b>don't help with the OT</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ set aside \"the originals\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1202.65,"lv":3,"h":"The Holy Spirit guided the whole process","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Even those folks say the same Holy Spirit who inspired also <b>guided preservation</b> for thousands of years</li><li class=\"d1\">active in copying, preserving, making sure the church <b>picked the right ones</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But \"errors crept in\" = Spirit tried to keep it perfect and <b>somehow failed</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Fr S: other direction - the Spirit is <b>God</b>; <b>whatever God sets out to do, He does</b></li><li class=\"d1\">whatever happened to the text was <b>exactly what the Spirit wanted</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No \"perfect version lost in the mists of history\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>every generation</b> of God's people has had the scriptures God wanted them to have - back to the Torah-only days</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. John Chrysostom's</b> NT text <b>was</b> the NT for him</li></ul>"},{"t":1317.46,"lv":2,"h":"What we have today is a gift","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Today: <b>6,000 NT manuscripts</b>, Greek/Hebrew/Aramaic OT versions, computer tools</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>big gift</b> previous generations didn't have</li><li class=\"d0\">Printing press - \"three and four Bibles <b>gathering dust</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">literacy above <b>single digits</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So: embrace what we have, <b>be thankful, study it</b> - not defend by digging for hypothetical perfect versions</li><li class=\"d0\">If 2 Cor looks like <b>two letters edited together</b> → the Spirit wanted that material handed down</li><li class=\"d1\">the first letter and the sorrowful letter: <b>God didn't want us to have them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if found tomorrow, we <b>wouldn't put them in the Bible</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1430.79,"lv":3,"h":"Why he raised it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Nobody in the room may be bothered - but recordings go <b>far and wide</b></li><li class=\"d0\">People on <b>History Channel, National Geographic, NPR</b> use this to <b>discredit the Bible</b> or plant doubt</li><li class=\"d1\">those who think Paul sat and wrote exactly what's in front of us</li><li class=\"d0\">Doubts pile up → people lose faith or wander off</li><li class=\"d0\">Hopefully helps anyone listening for whom it would be a problem</li></ul>"},{"t":1494.84,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians 1:1-2 - Greeting","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:1</b> \"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and <b>Timothy</b> our brother, to the church of God at Corinth, with all the saints in all <b>Achaia</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Achaia</b> = Roman province containing Corinth, roughly Greece</li><li class=\"d0\">Expects letter to be <b>passed around</b> to other churches, not read once and filed</li><li class=\"d1\">later <b>collection</b> wouldn't have struck him as odd</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Apostle</b> = <i>apostolos</i>, one sent, with a mission</li><li class=\"d1\">OT Greek translates Hebrew <i>shaliach</i> = sent as <b>representative</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>power of attorney</b>/lawyer - negotiates contract, business - \"my personal representative\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's self-understanding: <b>authority from Christ himself</b> to resolve matters at Corinth</li></ul>"},{"t":1703.47,"lv":3,"h":"Apostle, the gospel and Christ's coming","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ties to the <b>visit</b> in 1 Cor: key to Paul = <b>resurrection and return of Christ</b> (1 Cor 15)</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Evangelion</i> usually <b>plural</b> in ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">emperor's/general's <i>evangelia</i> = triumphs and victories <b>read out publicly before his visit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">city hears who is coming → <b>prepares</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul preaching Christ's <i>evangelion</i> = his victories → they prepare for his coming → <b>good visit, not bad</b></li><li class=\"d0\">End of 1 Cor: <b>Christ is coming soon</b> - be ready</li><li class=\"d0\">\"By the will of <b>God</b>\" = <b>God the Father</b> (reiterated in <b>ch. 6</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">partly Trinitarian - Spirit not mentioned here</li></ul>"},{"t":1859.78,"lv":2,"h":"Timothy as co-author","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Which epistles did Paul write? <b>Seven</b> everyone agrees; others doubted</li><li class=\"d0\">Answer often right in front of us: <b>co-author named</b> at the start, usually <b>Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek a bit different → \"because there was this other person involved\" - too simple for a dissertation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hebrews</b> etc. - Greek looks like <b>St. Luke's</b> (Gospel, Acts) - later</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't know how much input Timothy had - maybe just the writing</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Tertius</b> at end of Romans - \"I Tertius write this\" - means <b>third</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fathers numbered sons: Primus, Secundus, Tertius</li><li class=\"d0\">Timothy: sent ahead to Corinth in 1 Cor → knows the situation, they know him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:2</b> grace and peace from <b>God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2061.76,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians 1:3-4 - God of all comfort","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:3-4</b> \"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the <b>Father of mercies</b> and God of <b>all comfort</b>...\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Starts with <b>suffering, troubles, tribulations</b> - different tenor from 1 Cor</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor: more confident - \"I'm hearing bad reports, here's what to do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"water under the bridge\"; Paul and Timothy been through stuff with them</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Focus on comfort</b> - not \"what is wrong with you people?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"let's come back together\" and work through it</li><li class=\"d0\">Comforted by God → able to <b>pass comfort on</b> to others in trouble</li><li class=\"d0\">First his <b>authority</b>; now the other direction: <b>\"I've been comforted\"</b> - wants to pass on the peace</li></ul>"},{"t":2183.18,"lv":3,"h":"The Father is merciful","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Western idea: <b>Father = angry judge</b>, Jesus = merciful, interceding to calm him</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Father really mad at us\"; Jesus stops him punishing</li><li class=\"d0\">Not Paul: Father of <b>all mercies</b>, merciful, compassionate</li><li class=\"d0\">No juxtaposition - not \"mean Old Testament God\" vs \"friendly New Testament God\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2245.03,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians 1:5-6 - Sharing Christ's sufferings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:5</b> \"as the sufferings of Christ abound in us, so our consolation also abounds through Christ\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Our sufferings = <b>partaking in Christ's sufferings</b> - that's their meaning</li><li class=\"d1\">Corinthians' struggles to live as Christians: <b>not random or bad luck</b></li><li class=\"d1\">following Christ = <b>same road he walked</b>: rejection, suffering, grief, sorrow</li><li class=\"d0\">Also share the <b>consolation</b> - Christ didn't just die in pain, he <b>rose</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:6</b> afflicted → for <b>your consolation and salvation</b>; comforted → same</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's suffering <b>over the Corinth struggle</b> is for their help to endure</li><li class=\"d1\">any consolation he receives he <b>passes on</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2368.13,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A and Paul's posture","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Was he in prison, on his way home - specific things?</li><li class=\"d1\">A: he's talking about suffering <b>for their sakes</b>, experience <b>with them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Opposite of how we'd take it: Fr S as priest would <b>blame and dislike</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"whatever I'm going through, I want it to help me help <b>you</b> resolve this\"</li><li class=\"d1\">consolation and peace from God → pass on</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul again: a <b>representative with authority</b></li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor had a <b>veiled threat</b> (\"good visit\"); not this time</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I'm on your side\" - not here to mediate <b>God's judgment</b> but <b>God's mercy, consolation, comfort</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for <b>healing, repentance, restoration</b> after everything</li></ul>"}]});
